# TEMPO — Cursor Project Rules

You are building TEMPO, a music project management web app for a solo musician.
The original product specification lives in `tempo-design-spec.md` in the repo root.
Next-generation planning lives in these living documents (also repo root) — consult
them in addition to the design spec before building any feature:
- `FEATURE-SPECS.md` — planned feature areas, flows, non-goals
- `TECHNICAL-ARCHITECTURE.md` — client/server boundaries, query keys, routes
- `DATA-MODEL.md` — current vs proposed schema (additive migrations only)
- `DESIGN-SYSTEM-V2.md` — track workspace layout and interaction hierarchy
- `SPECTRA-TITLE-LANGUAGE.md` — title → Spectra light-slit visual language (placeholder covers)
- `IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md` — work-package order, migration gates, verification
- `SECURITY-AND-PERMISSIONS.md` — threat model and role matrix
- `TESTING-STRATEGY.md` — manual regression and future automation notes
- `TEAM-OPERATIONS-PRODUCT-SPEC.md`, `TEAM-OPERATIONS-UX-SPEC.md`,
  `TEAM-OPERATIONS-TECHNICAL-DESIGN.md`,
  `TEAM-OPERATIONS-SECURITY-AND-PERMISSIONS.md`, and
  `TEAM-OPERATIONS-IMPLEMENTATION-TEST-ROLLOUT-PLAN.md` — the current next
  initiative; specification only until individual packages ship
- `SESSIONS-V2-PRODUCT-SPEC.md`, `SESSIONS-V2-UX-DESIGN.md`,
  `SESSIONS-V2-TECHNICAL-DESIGN.md`, `CALLS-PLATFORM-DESIGN.md`,
  `SESSIONS-V2-SECURITY-AND-PERMISSIONS.md`, and
  `SESSIONS-V2-IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md` — rebuilding Sessions around the song it
  is about, plus calling in every message thread; specification only until
  individual packages ship. `SESSIONS-V2-KICKOFF-PROMPT.md` is the prompt to
  start a package from.
`tempo-design-spec.md` plus these documents are the source of truth for scope,
data model, screens, and visual design. `PRODUCT.md` describes only what exists
today — never treat roadmap specs as shipped product.

## Stack (fixed — do not substitute)
- Next.js 14+ App Router, TypeScript strict mode
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components
- Supabase (Postgres, Auth via email + password, Storage bucket `audio`)
- TanStack React Query for data fetching/caching
- wavesurfer.js for audio playback/waveforms
- @dnd-kit for Kanban drag and drop
- three (from npm) ONLY for the shader components in tempo-design-spec.md §5

Do not add other dependencies without asking. No Redux, no Prisma, no CSS-in-JS.

## Design system (from spec §5)

The identity below is fixed. The *depth layer* added in v0.13 (elevation,
larger panel radius, gradient surfaces, gradient scrims) is an extension —
see `DESIGN-SYSTEM-V2.md` §0 for the added tokens and utilities, and prefer
`.panel` / `.panel-quiet` / `.well` over hand-rolling card chrome.

Fixed (do not change without asking):
- Dark UI only. Use the CSS variables defined in `app/globals.css`
  (--bg-0 #0A0A0C, --bg-1 #121216, --bg-2 #1A1A21, --bg-3 #1F1F27,
   --line #26262E,
   --text-hi #F2F0EB, --text-lo #8B8B96, --ice #7FB4FF, --amber #FFB56B,
   --violet #9D8CFF, --ok #6FD99A, --warn #FF7A6B).
- Fonts (v0.16 — two-family system): Space Grotesk (display: titles, the
  wordmark) and Inter (everything else — body copy AND all musical/technical
  data: BPM, keys, timestamps, version numbers, durations, counts). JetBrains
  Mono was dropped; do not reintroduce a third typeface without asking — the
  user explicitly asked to go from three fonts down to two because inconsistent
  weight/tracking on font-display was reading as "too many fonts." Tailwind's
  `font-mono` and the `.font-data` utility both resolve to Inter now.
- Ice = interactive (buttons, links, focus). Amber = status/emphasis
  ("current" markers, momentum, flare-line centers). Never both as CTAs together.
- Radius: 10px cards, 8px inputs, 999px chips; 16px (--radius-panel) for major
  surfaces only. 1px --line borders. Elevation via --shadow-1/2/3 is allowed;
  never nest .panel inside .panel (shadows compound) — use .well for inner rows.
- Signature motif: .flare-line (1px horizontal gradient
  transparent → ice → white → amber → transparent) for dividers/active markers.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion everywhere; shader components must pause
  when offscreen/hidden and have static gradient fallbacks.

## Database rules — CRITICAL
- The production database is live Supabase with the user's real music data.
- NEVER drop, truncate, or recreate tables. NEVER suggest "resetting" the DB.
- Schema changes go in numbered files in /migrations (e.g. 002_add_x.sql)
  for the user to run manually in the Supabase SQL editor. Tell them when
  a migration needs running.
- versions rows: never UPDATE file_url. Keep at most 2 versions per track —
  after upload, auto-delete older ones (and their storage files). Manual
  delete still requires a strong confirm.
- version_no and is_current are managed by DB triggers — do not set version_no
  in application code.
- Completion % is always computed from checklist_items, never stored.

## Storage rules
- All file access goes through `lib/storage.ts` (single module) so the
  provider can be swapped later. Upload paths:
  tracks/{track_id}/versions/{version_id}/{filename}
  tracks/{track_id}/assets/{asset_id}/{filename}
- Playback/download uses signed URLs with 1-hour expiry. The `audio` bucket
  is private; never make it public.

## Production URL
- Live app: https://mytempo.dev (sign-in at /login).
- Auto-deployed from `main` via Vercel.
- Use this URL for absolute links that ship (auth redirects, PWA manifest
  `start_url`, etc.). Never hardcode localhost in anything that ships.

## Secrets
- Supabase URL and anon key live ONLY in .env.local (gitignored) and Vercel
  env vars. Never hardcode them, never print them, never commit .env.local.

## Working style
- Small scoped changes; finish the current work package before expanding scope.
- After each work package, output a short manual test checklist.
- Optimistic UI updates for drag-and-drop and checkbox toggles.
- Mobile-responsive per spec §6 (rail collapses to bottom tab bar).
- Plain, studio-casual UI copy per spec §5 ("Upload a bounce", "Log a session").

## Web + desktop parity — REQUIRED
Read `docs/WEB-DESKTOP-RELEASE-POLICY.md` before changing desktop-sensitive
behavior or anything under `electron/`.

- The Electron renderer loads the production Next.js app. Keep product UI and
  business logic in the shared web app; never duplicate it under `electron/`.
- Classify each change as shared-web, desktop-native, or cross-boundary.
- Test desktop-sensitive web changes in both a browser and the Electron shell.
- **Mac ↔ Windows desktop parity is required.** Shared `electron/` behavior
  (URLs, OAuth, vault, updater, bridge) must ship for both platforms in the
  same desktop version. Never land a Windows-only or Mac-only customer fix
  without the matching platform path, and verify Desktop Release published
  both `TEMPO-Setup.exe` and `TEMPO-Mac.dmg` for that version.
- Web code must capability-detect native bridge features and keep a safe
  fallback for older desktop installs.
- Cross-boundary changes ship native-first, then web. Never require a web
  deploy and desktop update to land simultaneously.
- Changes under `electron/` require a desktop version bump, a CHANGELOG entry,
  `npm run check:desktop-release`, and a published desktop release after merge.
- Never publish customer updates from the private source repository. Use the
  public binary-only release channel defined in the policy document.

## App version — REQUIRED after every change
The release version shown under Settings in the left rail lives in
`lib/version.ts` as `APP_VERSION`, and must match `version` in `package.json`.

Bump BOTH on every work package and every later change request (feature,
fix, behavior, or design). Skipping this is failing the task.

Semver for TEMPO (still 0.x):
- Patch (0.2.0 → 0.2.1): bug fix or tiny tweak with no user-facing feature.
- Minor (0.2.0 → 0.3.0): new feature or meaningful behavior/UI change.
- Do not bump major until the product leaves 0.x.

Mention the new version in the CHANGELOG entry for that change
(e.g. a bullet or parenthetical like "v0.2.1").

## Living documentation — REQUIRED after every change
Two documents in the repo root must stay current. Update them as the FINAL
step of every work package AND every later change request (feature added,
feature removed, behavior changed, design changed). Skipping this is failing
the task. Pure bug fixes with no behavior change may be logged in one line.

1. CHANGELOG.md — a plain-English, reverse-chronological log for the product
   owner (a musician, not a developer). Each entry: a date heading (## YYYY-MM-DD)
   and bullets in the form "Added / Changed / Removed / Fixed: <what it means for
   the user>" — e.g. "Added: you can now drag tracks between stages on the
   board", never "refactored KanbanColumn props". One optional "Under the hood"
   line only if the user must act (e.g. "run migration 003 in Supabase before
   deploying").

2. PRODUCT.md — the overarching living description of TEMPO: what it is, who
   it's for, and its current feature set in natural language. It describes the
   product AS IT EXISTS TODAY — not the roadmap. When a feature ships, fold it
   into the relevant section in flowing prose; when something changes or is
   removed, rewrite that part so the document never describes anything untrue.
   A stranger reading only PRODUCT.md should accurately understand the product.

Both documents are written in plain language for a non-programmer. No file
paths, component names, or library names unless the user must interact with
them.

## Live deployment
The production app is live at https://mytempo.dev (sign-in at
/login). Every push to main auto-deploys there via Vercel. Use this URL for
absolute links (auth redirects, PWA manifest start_url) and when telling the
user where to verify changes. Never hardcode localhost in anything that ships.

## Concurrent multi-agent work — REQUIRED
Multiple people and multiple agents (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, others) work
on TEMPO at the same time. **Do not share one checkout for writes.** Use the
**three-slot workspace pool** before any edit:

1. **Acquire** a slot: `node scripts/agent-workspace.mjs acquire --wait --json`
   (or `npm run agent:workspace`). Slots are **Workspace 1**, **Workspace 2**,
   **Workspace 3** under `../TEMPO-worktrees/`. If all three are busy,
   `--wait` blocks on the pool mutex until one frees.
2. **Move** Cursor to that root (`move_agent_to_root`) before editing.
3. Work inside that workspace only. The primary `TEMPO` folder is for the
   human and the guarded integration command — never edit it directly.
4. **Stage narrowly** — never `git add -A`.
5. Finish the version bump, CHANGELOG, and PRODUCT.md in the slot, then run
   typecheck + tests.
6. **Do not commit or push unless the user explicitly asks.**
7. **Finish locally** with
   `node scripts/agent-workspace.mjs release --agent-id <id>`. Release first
   integrates the slot into the primary local `TEMPO` checkout, then cleans and
   frees the slot. It never pushes.
8. If primary `TEMPO` is dirty, not on `main`, or conflicts, integration must
   stop and preserve both checkouts. Never auto-stash or overwrite human work.
9. Cloud/background agents cannot access the local checkout. They use a remote
   branch or PR; the next foreground local session fast-forwards local `TEMPO`.

Never create extra worktrees beyond the three named slots. See `AGENTS.md` and
`.cursor/rules/agent-workspaces.mdc` for acquire/release, heartbeats, and
force-clear.
